I fixed #1151 and committed it as 2a3612fc. yoyo --system --model claude-zzz -p hi is still refused with exit code 2 and nothing sent, but the error now names the real cause:

```
error: --system took '--model' as its value, so 'claude-zzz' (meant for --model) was left over. Give --system its own value (--system "..." --model claude-zzz). Nothing was sent to the model.
```

The old fix it suggested, -p "hi claude-zzz", would have sent the model name to the model as prompt text. That suggestion is gone.

Build, fmt check, clippy with -D warnings and the full cargo test all pass. One full test run earlier counted 2 FAILED/panicked lines. I couldn't reproduce them in two more full runs and don't know which test they came from, so there may be a flaky test somewhere.

What changed
• The check: a new helper, swallowed_flag_values, in src/cli_config.rs spots a flag that took another flag as its value (here --system took --model). It only counts flags that themselves need a value, and the bare word right after them is the one blamed on the swallowed flag. It reuses the existing option_positions walk, so there's no second argv parser. src/cli.rs is unchanged, still at 7861 lines.
• Mixed cases: if an argv has both kinds of stray word, the "took --model" explanation comes first. The quote-the-whole-prompt suggestion is then offered only for the genuine extra prompt words.
• Tests:
  • A unit test for the #1151 argv and the mixed case, plus three cases that must still blame -p:
    • -p hi extra (the #1150 case), whose message is unchanged.
    • --system --verbose -p hi x: --verbose takes no value, so nothing was left over by it.
    • --system -terse -p hi x: a dash-led system prompt is a real value.
  • A real-binary test in tests/flag_value_slot.rs: exit 2, --system named, no -p "hi claude-zzz", and the stub receives no request.
• Positive control: I disabled the new helper (with the marker), ran the tests, restored it and ran them again, all in one command. Both new tests failed by name and passed once restored.

A choice you might disagree with: the issue's done-when says any "dash-led word", but its predicate line says "a KNOWN_FLAGS entry". I went with the narrower one, a known flag that takes a value, so a system prompt that starts with a dash is never mistaken for a swallowed flag. I didn't reuse require_flag_value's "looks like a flag" check because it treats any dash-led word as a flag and would misfire on that case.

The commit body has the before/after map for each of the five argv words, and none of them is dropped without a message. No docs page shows this error text, so no doc edits were needed.
